 of serious  birth defect  but she worries that many are not  receiving critical follow-up care we  lose about a quarter of those families  after delivery where they go back to  their community hospital their community  pediatrician and they don't stay engaged  with our care and that means we don't  have any data on what's happening with  them in South Florida crews are hitting  the ground early using larvacide to kill  mosquitoes before they hatch  last season miami-dade county air  dropped the controversial pesticide  naled in several citizens and residents  protested hey hey we don't want no spray  miami-dade Mayor Carlos Gimenez hopes it  won't come to that again this year  they've beefed up the mosquito control  budget and are pushing community  awareness if you don't plan on having  you know a child etc it's still it's  your personal responsibility to make  sure you don't become a transmitter to  somebody that does that's where our  message is here you know all of us have  to take care of all of us but not every  homeowner is up to speed you thought it  was gone that's going give wow so make a  gun it's not gone yes I did only be in  si mi siento Finley  Jessica Flores told us she went public  to promote awareness so that other  pregnant women can avoid the stress and  anxiety  she suffered is this just something we  now have to live with is Zika ever gonna  go away we live in a time of Zika and  this is something that we are going to  deal with this season and maybe next  season ultimately until there's either  treatment vaccination or both this is  the world that we live in the Zika  vaccine is still in its second phase of  testing so it won't be available for  this season a recent report from  Stanford University suggested Zika can  be transmitted at a temperature of 84  degrees that is six degrees lower than  originally thought the high here in  Miami today is expected to be in the 90s  Dana ml Bojorquez Thank You Manuel
 

